      en: "Mount Thor on Baffin Island, Nunavut, features a near-vertical granite cliff that drops 1,250 m (4,101 ft), one of the highest vertical drops on Earth. The summit is at 1,675 m (5,495 ft). The first recorded ascent was in 1963 via the north face. Named after the Norse god Thor, the cliff is a challenge for extreme climbers due to Arctic conditions. It lies within Auyuittuq National Park. The cliff was formed by glacial erosion. Summer temperatures rarely exceed 10 °C.",
