      en: "Mount Rundle is a 2,948 m (9,672 ft) mountain in the Canadian Rockies, Alberta, overlooking the town of Banff. It stretches about 12 km (7.5 mi) long and is known for its nearly symmetrical profile. Composed mainly of sedimentary rock, including limestone and sandstone, it was named after Methodist missionary Robert Rundle. Popular for hiking and climbing, with routes like the Rundle Summit Trail. The eastern face features steep cliffs. Geologically, it belongs to the Rundle Group from the Paleozoic era. Mount Rundle is within Banff National Park.",
