      en: "The Baffin Mountains are a range on Baffin Island, Nunavut, Canada, extending about 1,000 km (620 mi). Part of the Arctic Cordillera, they contain some of the highest peaks in eastern North America, including Mount Odin (2,147 m) and Mount Asgard (2,015 m). Heavily glaciated and dissected by deep fjords, they are composed of Precambrian rock of the Canadian Shield. The climate is arctic, with long, cold winters. Wildlife includes polar bears, caribou, and arctic foxes. The mountains are a destination for expedition climbing. Inuit have inhabited the region for thousands of years.",
