      en: "Mount Sir Donald is a prominent peak in the Selkirk Mountains of British Columbia, Canada, named after Donald Alexander Smith, 1st Baron Strathcona and Mount Royal. It is part of Glacier National Park, renowned for its steep rock faces and glaciers. First ascended in 1890 by a party led by J. H. Scattergood, the mountain rises to 3,275 meters (10,745 feet) above sea level. Geologically, it consists mainly of metamorphic rock and granite. The peak is located in the drainage basin of the Illecillewaet River and is surrounded by several smaller glaciers. Its challenging routes attract experienced mountaineers, and the area supports wildlife such as mountain goats and grizzly bears.",
